# Junior Sprinters: Short Jumps Trained Stride, Long Jumps Trained Top Speed, and the Combination Was Called a Summation

**What it is** — "In an experiment with junior class sprinters" three groups trained "for a period of eight months": one did mainly short jumps (repeated jumps on one and two legs, triple and quintuple standing broad jumps), one mainly long jumping exercises ("multiple jumps on one leg or from leg to leg over distances of 30-100m"), and one did both (PDF pp. 299-300 / printed pp. 288-289). The authors report: "The short jumping exercises developed primarily length and speed of the running stride, whereas the long jumping exercises largely increased maximum running speed and speed-strength endurance. The combination of short and long jumping exercises led to a summation of their separate training effects and the development of speed-strength abilities which are specific to sprinters" (PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289). Fig 5.12 (verified against the page image) plots the decrease in sprint time in seconds for each group at 30 m and 60 m from a low start (LS) and a standing start (SS), and at 100 m from a low start. Approximate bar heights read from the image (short / long / combined): 30 m LS about 0.40 / 0.08 / 0.29 s; 30 m SS about 0.07 / 0.13 / 0.20 s; 60 m LS about 0.29 / 0.22 / 0.33 s; 60 m SS about 0.08 / 0.37 / 0.43 s; 100 m LS about 0.27 / 0.50 / 1.0 s (the 100 m panel uses a 0 to 1.0 s scale). No group sizes or statistics are given.

**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism beyond the outcome labels: short jumps act on stride length and stride speed, long jumps on maximum speed and speed-strength endurance, and the two "summate." Inference: the split matches the jump durations (brief standing multi-jumps vs bounding over 30-100 m), but the text does not draw that link.

**When to apply** — Choosing jump means for sprint development: the authors' data suggest short, standing multi-jumps and long bounding series train different parts of the sprint, so a sprint-focused off-season can include both. Inference: for football, short jumps map to the start and early acceleration distances and long bounds to the longer runs, but the authors tested junior track sprinters, not football players.

**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (text vs figure).** The text calls the combined effect "a summation of their separate training effects," but in Fig 5.12 the short-jump group, not the combined group, shows the largest drop at 30 m from a low start (about 0.40 vs 0.29 s). The combined group is largest at the other four distances. Read against the sum of the two single groups, the combined bar is about equal to it at 30 m SS (0.20 vs 0.07 + 0.13) and 60 m SS (0.43 vs 0.08 + 0.37), above it at 100 m LS (1.0 vs 0.27 + 0.50), and below it at 60 m LS (0.33 vs 0.29 + 0.22). So "summation" fits some distances and not others. The bar values above are read by eye from a printed chart and are approximate. The subjects were junior sprinters; the authors give no data for senior athletes.

**Example** — The authors' own: over 100 m from a low start, the combined jump group improved by about 1.0 s, against about 0.27 s for short jumps alone and about 0.50 s for long jumps alone (Fig 5.12, PDF p. 300 / printed p. 289).

**Related** — [[heuristic--sequence-jumps-then-barbell-then-depth-jumps-across-stages]], [[framework--sequential-and-concurrent-variants-of-systematic-means]] (the concurrent variant this experiment illustrates), [[claim--400m-stride-length-held-by-local-strength-endurance]], [[claim--absolute-leg-strength-connects-to-sprint-speed-only-indirectly-via-jump]], [[fundamentals-of-sprinting--moyer/framework--soviet-lineage-of-sprint-training-ideas]]
