# Traditional Circuit Weight Training Gives Only Modest Mixed Benefits, Because Removing Rest Removes Heavy, Explosive and Stretching Work

**What it is** — The authors' verdict on the "average commercial circuit": "only a moderate compromise of the reputed cardiovascular and strength conditioning benefits can be achieved by traditional circuit training" (PDF pp. 311-312 / printed pp. 300-301). Specifically: circuit weight training (CWT) "using combinations of gymnasium apparatus with fixed bicycles or mini-trampolines cannot produce cardiovascular conditioning which rivals that of running, cycling and other traditional forms"; "because CWT generally relies on the use of light weights and high repetitions, increase in strength and power is also very limited"; and "Virtually the only significant benefits of the average commercial circuit are a modest development of local muscular endurance, moderate decrease in percentage bodyfat and a mild improvement in cardiovascular fitness." "Alternation of conventional weight training on one day and endurance training on another day produces superior results to traditional circuit training in terms of improvements in strength, and cardiovascular stamina" (PDF p. 312 / printed p. 301, checked against the page image). Circuit training is dated to "R Morgan and G Adamson in 1953 at the University of Leeds" (PDF p. 311 / printed p. 300, checked against the page image).

**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the rest interval. Circuit training "operates on the assumption that it can simultaneously condition you aerobically and anaerobically," and since "any rest intervals will tend to prevent the body from operating in the aerobic conditioning zone," it allows no rest beyond moving between stations (PDF p. 312 / printed p. 301). "Paradoxically, its major limitations are imposed by its very attempt to introduce aerobic conditioning into conventional weight training by eliminating rest intervals. The absence of rest periods prevents one from using heavy weights and developing strength, from holding static or resistance-assisted stretches to enhance flexibility, and from performing intensive explosive movements to improve speed" (PDF p. 313 / printed p. 302, checked against the page image). Underneath is specificity: "if one wishes to enhance strength, one has to do high intensity strength training with a minimum of prolonged aerobic training, since the latter tends to decrease strength" (PDF p. 312 / printed p. 301).

**When to apply** — When a circuit is proposed as an all-in-one strength and conditioning session, expect modest gains in each quality; for athletes who need real strength and real aerobic fitness, train them on separate days. Eric's baseline: "A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once" and priority has to be picked from what competition demands (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 115, Explicit; quoted up to the line's dash, the rest paraphrased). Inference: the authors' rejection of "simultaneous maximum conditioning" (PDF p. 312) is the same priority logic applied to strength and endurance together.

**When NOT to apply** — The authors cite no study for any finding in this passage ("It has been shown," "A literature review reveals"), so `supported` is a ceiling. The verdict is on "traditional" or "average commercial" circuits; the authors go on to argue that interval circuits with rest restored are far more useful (see Related), and they say "This is not to state that circuit training is of little value" (PDF p. 312 / printed p. 301). One figure on PDF p. 313 / printed p. 302 is unclear: conventional training rest of 2-4 minutes is said to be common "for few repetitions (1-3) with loads close to one's 1RM or a fairly large number of repetitions (10-15) with over 80% of one's 1RM." The image confirms the wording, but 10-15 repetitions at over 80% of 1RM is not possible for most lifters; treat that number as unclear. The note that "heavier individuals require longer recovery periods than their lighter colleagues" has no citation.

**Example** — The authors' own: CWT combining gym apparatus with fixed bicycles or mini-trampolines did not match the cardiovascular conditioning of running or cycling, and alternating weight-training days with endurance days beat it for both strength and stamina (PDF p. 312 / printed p. 301).

**Related** — [[claim--continuous-vs-interval-circuit-training-for-concurrent-goals]] (Ch1, the authors' earlier CCT vs ICT verdict and fibre-type argument), [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]], [[heuristic--circuits-cannot-develop-sport-specific-strength-qualities]], [[principle--local-muscular-endurance-adaptation-is-specific-to-the-trained-muscles]], [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--concurrent-training-interference-and-decay]]
@@@@@ framework--aerobic-vs-anaerobic-circuit-parameters.md
