# Aerobic vs Anaerobic Circuits: Rough Guidelines for Reps, Load, Rest and Laps

**What it is** — The authors give "rough guidelines," warning that "a considerable degree of individuality is involved and no precise figures can be quoted to be universally appropriate for everyone" (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304, checked against the page image). Cardiovascular circuits "all fall under the category of CCT," and "A true aerobic circuit would involve the predominant use of very low resistance devices or prolonged rhythmic movements"; anaerobic circuits "may be either CCT (continuous) or ICT (interval), with the former resulting largely in muscle endurance, and variations of the latter developing strength, power, muscle endurance, speed or flexibility."

| | Cardiovascular (aerobic) circuits | Anaerobic circuits |
|---|---|---|
| Rest | "Continuous activity or very brief intervals" between stations | "Rest intervals between stations to permit some recovery (1-4 minutes)" |
| Station mix | aerobic activity alternating "between resistance stations" | "Fewer aerobic stations and more resistance stations" |
| Reps per resistance station | "20-40" | "3-8" |
| Load | "usually not more than 30% of 1RM" | "usually more than 70% of 1RM" |
| Laps ("excursions") | "5-10" | "3-5" |

Sub-cases (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304): for dynamic muscle endurance, "resistance should be nearer 50% of 1RM, repetitions should be nearer 50, number of excursions should be near 4 and rest intervals should be shorter"; for strength and power, "resistance should exceed 75% of 1RM, repetitions should not exceed 5, rest intervals should be nearer 2 minutes and there should be no more than 3 excursions"; "Development of power generally requires the smallest number of repetitions"; flexibility circuits "may require static stretches to be held for 20-30 seconds." Agility circuits "necessitate many repetitions to imprint the correct movement patterns," and restoration circuits must be "of low intensity, moderate duration and low complexity," using patterns "such as those used in physiotherapeutic PNF or Tai Chi" (PDF p. 316 / printed p. 305, checked against the page image). A second load scheme, "applicable to the average programme designed to moderately enhance muscle endurance and cardiovascular performance," uses "40-50% of 10RM for beginners, 40-50% of 5RM for intermediate trainees, 40-50% of 1RM for advanced trainees," and "For elite athletes or strength circuits, as much as 80% of the 1RM may be used" (PDF p. 317 / printed p. 306, checked against the page image).

**Why it works** — The authors tie the split to rest: continuous work keeps the circuit aerobic, while rest allows heavier loads and fewer reps for strength, power and speed (PDF pp. 312-313, 315 / printed pp. 301-302, 304). They give no further mechanism and cite no study; they refer readers to Table 1.1 "for further information" (PDF p. 315).

**When to apply** — Setting first-draft numbers for a group circuit, then adjusting for the individual. Eric's baseline: "Aerobic-system training is kept below anaerobic threshold with roughly a 1:1 to 1:2 work:rest ratio and a general speed ceiling around 4.5 m/s, specifically to avoid spilling over into glycolytic-system stress." (`docs/method-baseline.md`, line 124, Explicit). Inference: the authors' cardiovascular circuit has no work:rest rule at all, so for football the baseline's work:rest and speed caps would be the binding limits.

**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (three load figures for muscle-endurance circuits).** The anaerobic column says 3-8 reps at over 70% of 1RM; the muscle-endurance sub-case of the same anaerobic category says about 50% of 1RM for about 50 reps (the image confirms "50"); and PDF p. 317 gives 40-50% of 10RM, 5RM or 1RM for programmes aimed at muscle endurance and cardiovascular performance. Fifty reps at 50% of 1RM also falls outside both columns of the table. The text gives no way to reconcile them. All percentage figures assume a tested max (1RM, 5RM or 10RM). The authors' own conclusion limits circuits to non-athletes and the early preparatory phase (PDF p. 323 / printed p. 312), which sits uneasily with "strength circuits" at over 75% of 1RM; see [[heuristic--circuits-cannot-develop-sport-specific-strength-qualities]].

**Example** — The authors' own strength-and-power circuit: over 75% of 1RM, no more than 5 reps, rest nearer 2 minutes, no more than 3 laps (PDF p. 315 / printed p. 304).

**Related** — [[framework--continuous-interval-and-mixed-circuit-training-classification]], [[heuristic--circuit-progression-load-prescription-and-novice-pre-testing]], [[framework--resistance-training-prescription-by-goal]], [[claim--traditional-circuit-weight-training-gives-only-modest-mixed-benefits]], [[heuristic--setchinov-alternation-for-circuit-station-sequencing]]
@@@@@ framework--circuit-layout-variants-superset-retrophase-zigzag-detour.md
---
